---
/**
 * foodcraft / HowBL — "Kalinaw Coffee" HOW IT WORKS (design element
 * data-component="HOW-IT-WORKS").
 *
 * ROLE: the page's one procedural block, and the FIRST of the template's two
 * full-bleed ESPRESSO bands. On a café with no ordering system this is the only
 * place that says how an order is actually placed — in the owner's words, never
 * ours. The design draws it as espresso ground between the gallery and the
 * testimonials: amber eyebrow, one Fraunces headline in bone, three equal
 * columns each opened by a SOLID AMBER 42px disc carrying a two-digit numeral,
 * and a single amber button under them.
 *
 * DESIGN VALUES, lifted verbatim from "Kalinaw Coffee Landing.dc.html":
 *   band      background #241A12                              → .band-dark
 *   margin    96px above the band (the gallery above it ends bare)
 *   padding   88px 0
 *   eyebrow   10px / .42em caps / #E8A33C 600                  → .kick on a band
 *   h2        Fraunces 400, clamp(31px,4vw,54px)/1.08,
 *             margin .26em 0 50px, #FBF6EC, max-width 24ch     → .h2 on a band
 *   grid      repeat(3, minmax(0,1fr)), gap 42px
 *   disc      42x42, radius 50%, FILLED #E8A33C, label #241A12,
 *             Fraunces 17px, flex-centred                      → .fig + .how-num
 *   h3        Fraunces 400, 25px, margin 18px 0 10px, #FBF6EC
 *   body      15px / 1.75 / #B8A795, margin 0                  → .body on a band
 *   action    margin-top 50px, btnAmber = pill + amber fill +
 *             #241A12 label, 12px caps, 16px 32px              → .btn.btn--band-accent
 * Every one of those colours is a wrapper token (R1); not one hex is written
 * here. btnAmber is character-for-character the wrapper's .btn + .btn--band-accent, so
 * this file adds NO button rule at all — only the full width and the wrapped
 * label at 520px.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (all declared in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts,
 * group "how" — a data-field on any other path is a dead hook no input owns)
 *   how.tag                  — the amber eyebrow
 *   how.headline             — set:html, so an owner can place the template's
 *                              signature italic emphasis anywhere inside their
 *                              own sentence. Guarded: derive-content-defaults
 *                              supplies NO how.headline, so an unset value must
 *                              draw nothing, not a gap above the grid
 *   how.steps.${i}.title
 *   how.steps.${i}.body      — canonical key, spelled in the hook even when the
 *                              stored value arrived as .desc / .description, so
 *                              the first edit heals the row onto the schema key
 *   hero.cta1.text           — the amber button's label
 *   hero.cta1.href           — its target. Deliberately the page's ONE primary
 *                              action reused, not a fourth CTA field: the schema
 *                              declares hero.cta1.text as a `link` FieldSpec
 *                              whose hrefPath is hero.cta1.href, so both halves
 *                              are owned by a real sidebar input
 *
 * DERIVED, not content:
 *   The amber disc numeral. It counts DISPLAYED steps (01, 02, 03 — never a
 *   gap), is aria-hidden and carries no data-field, so the design's demo `st.n`
 *   can never become a content key an owner has to maintain. The list is a real
 *   <ol>, so the ordinal reaches assistive tech through the markup rather than
 *   through a decorative glyph.
 *
 * ANTI-FABRICATION (R2). Not one word here is written by this file. The design's
 * own steps ("Send your order", "Mornings before 9am we are quickest to reply",
 * "your drinks go on the bar to hit your pickup minute") are the DESIGNER
 * filling in their own template — they are a reply-time promise and a service
 * guarantee made on behalf of a business nobody has spoken to, so none of them
 * ships. A café that has said nothing about its process gets no process band:
 * blank rows are dropped and an all-blank list removes the whole <section>.
 *
 * SECTION GATE (R5) = rows.length, the BODY content. It cannot count how.tag,
 * because derive-content-defaults hard-codes how.tag to 'How it works' on EVERY
 * site — a gate that counted it would ship an espresso band, an eyebrow and a
 * button over an empty grid on every site that never filled the steps in. The
 * CTA is not counted either: a band whose only content is a button is a heading
 * over nothing.
 *
 * LIST INDEXES (R6). data-field carries the ORIGINAL array index; the disc
 * carries the DISPLAY index. Dropping a blank row 2 therefore renumbers nothing
 * and cannot misdirect an edit of row 3 into row 2.
 *
 * ARRAY SOURCE. how.steps is canonical, and the hook ALWAYS spells how.steps —
 * the read-tolerant / write-canonical split every other How section in the repo
 * uses. A legacy site whose rows live under how.items still RENDERS, but its
 * hooks are not re-pointed at how.items: genericContentSchema declares no
 * how.items list and no fallbackPaths for it, so a hook spelled that way would
 * be inert in the sidebar. Spelling how.steps keeps the row editable and heals
 * it onto the canonical array on the first save.
 *
 * THE DISC IS THE ONE PLACE THIS FILE FIGHTS THE WRAPPER, and it is not an
 * oversight. PageBL's band rule `:where(.band-dark) .fig` paints every figure
 * BONE, which on the design's filled amber disc is about 1.7:1 — the numeral
 * would vanish. The design states the disc label as #241A12, so .how-num.fig
 * restates it as --band. The selector is deliberately two classes because the
 * band rule scores (0,2,1) and a single scoped class would lose to it.
 * The h3 has the same shape of problem: PageBL's shared heading reset paints
 * h1–h4 with --ink and the band rule only covers .h2, so a bare <h3> here would
 * be espresso ink on an espresso ground. It is restated as --bone, qualified
 * with the section class so it beats that (0,1,1) reset.
 *
 * NOT RENDERED, deliberately:
 *   - No id on the section. This page's anchors are menu / about / gallery /
 *     location / faq / main; the design gives HOW-IT-WORKS no id and no nav link
 *     points at it, so inventing #how would add a target nothing aims for.
 *   - No target="_blank" on an IN-PAGE action. The design hard-codes one because
 *     its demo href is an external messaging URL, but here the same button may
 *     resolve to '#location', and opening an in-page anchor in a new tab is a
 *     bug. It is emitted for http(s) ONLY — the same conversion HeaderBL,
 *     CtaBandBL and FooterBL make, and CtaBandBL's amber action is this exact
 *     button reading this exact field.
 *   - NOT dropped, though it is the value a reader is most likely to think was
 *     an oversight in reverse: the design's `margin-top:96px` IS carried. The
 *     section that renders directly above this band is GalleryBL, and it ships
 *     the design's bare bottom (`padding: clamp(56px,7.6vw,92px) 0 0`) and says
 *     in its own frontmatter that the espresso band below it owns the gap — so
 *     without a margin here the mosaic butts flush against the band's top edge.
 *     It is scaled on PageBL's own clamp curve. If the gallery is switched off,
 *     the section that takes its place keeps the shared symmetric `section`
 *     rhythm and the gap reads generous rather than broken, which is the right
 *     way round for the trade. The 88px the design asks for INSIDE the band is
 *     kept as well: a coloured ground must own its own inset.
 *   - No step count is asserted in the markup or the copy. The grid is three
 *     tracks because the design is three tracks, but two or five owner-typed
 *     steps lay out on the same tracks without a hole or a wrap.
 *
 * THEMING (R1/R4). --band-accent and the bones are NOT the theme engine's vocabulary
 * (see PageBL's frontmatter): they exist only on the espresso bands, chosen
 * against #241A12, so a scheme pick recolours the rust elsewhere and leaves this
 * band's disc, eyebrow and button legible exactly as drawn. The section class is
 * "how-band" and the button is .btn--band-accent (double dash): buildOverrideCss pins
 * label colours on .btn-primary / .btn-yellow / .nav-cta / .cta-band .btn /
 * .testi .btn with !important, and none of those selectors reaches anything here.
 *
 * SCOPING (R3). One scoped style block, no is:global. The headline is fed with
 * set:html and scoped rules cannot reach elements a set:html creates — which is
 * why there is no italic-emphasis rule in this file: PageBL already styles
 * `:where(.band-dark) .h2 em` to amber from its global block, so an owner's <em>
 * is emphasised there and a scoped duplicate here would be dead CSS.
 */
// `layout` is accepted because PageBL passes it to every section uniformly;
// this block reads nothing outside content.*.
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content } = Astro.props;

const how = content.how || {};
const tag = typeof how.tag === 'string' ? how.tag.trim() : '';
// Trimmed, and otherwise untouched: this goes out through set:html, so whatever
// markup the owner typed reaches the wrapper's em rule intact.
const headline = typeof how.headline === 'string' ? how.headline.trim() : '';

type Step = { title?: string; body?: string; desc?: string; description?: string };
// Read tolerantly — a legacy site stores its rows under how.items — and write
// canonically. See ARRAY SOURCE above.
const source: Step[] = Array.isArray(how.steps) ? how.steps : (Array.isArray(how.items) ? how.items : []);
const titleOf = (s: Step): string => String(s?.title ?? '').trim();
const bodyOf = (s: Step): string => String(s?.body ?? s?.desc ?? s?.description ?? '').trim();
// A step the admin added but never filled would otherwise print an amber disc
// over nothing, and a title of pure whitespace would ship an empty <h3>.
// Normalise first, drop the blanks (whitespace counts as blank) and keep the
// ORIGINAL index on every survivor.
const rows = source
  .map((s, i: number) => ({ i, title: titleOf(s), body: bodyOf(s) }))
  .filter((r) => Boolean(r.title || r.body));

// Anchor guard (R7). derive-content-defaults hard-codes hero.cta1.href to
// '#visit', an id this page does not render, so the mirrored CTA would scroll
// nowhere. Resolve in-page hrefs against the ids PageBL actually renders and
// keep this table in step with the copies in HeaderBL / HeroBL / CtaBandBL /
// FooterBL. Off-page hrefs (tel:, mailto:, http) pass straight through.
const PAGE_ANCHORS = new Set(['menu', 'about', 'gallery', 'location', 'faq', 'main']);
const ANCHOR_ALIAS: Record<string, string> = {
  book: 'main',
  contact: 'location',
  host: 'about',
  rooms: 'menu',
  services: 'menu',
  visit: 'location',
  why: 'about',
  work: 'gallery',
};
const onPage = (href: string | undefined, fallback: string): string => {
  if (!href) return fallback;
  if (!href.startsWith('#')) return href;
  const id = href.slice(1);
  if (PAGE_ANCHORS.has(id)) return href;
  const alias = ANCHOR_ALIAS[id];
  return alias && PAGE_ANCHORS.has(alias) ? `#${alias}` : fallback;
};

const hero = content.hero || {};
const ctaText = typeof hero.cta1?.text === 'string' ? hero.cta1.text.trim() : '';
// #location is where this template puts the address, the hours and the way to
// reach the café, so it is the honest landing place for an action that cannot
// be resolved — the step above it just told the visitor to get in touch.
const ctaHref = onPage(hero.cta1?.href, '#location');
// The design hard-codes target="_blank" rel="noopener" on this action because
// its own href is a Messenger URL. Carried for http(s) ONLY: the same button may
// now resolve to '#location', and opening an in-page anchor in a new tab is a
// bug. Same test, same idiom as HeaderBL / CtaBandBL / FooterBL.
const ctaExternal = /^https?:/i.test(ctaHref);
---
{rows.length > 0 && (
  <section class="how-band band-dark">
    <div class="wrap">
      {tag && <span class="kick" data-field="how.tag">{tag}</span>}
      {headline && <h2 class="h2 how-h2 reveal" data-field="how.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}

      <ol class="how-grid">
        {rows.map((step, k) => (
          <li class="how-step reveal" data-delay={String(Math.min(k, 3))}>
            {/* DERIVED chrome: display index, aria-hidden, no data-field. The
                <ol> already carries the ordinal for assistive tech. */}
            <span class="fig how-num" aria-hidden="true">{String(k + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
            {step.title && <h3 data-field={`how.steps.${step.i}.title`}>{step.title}</h3>}
            {step.body && <p class="body" data-field={`how.steps.${step.i}.body`}>{step.body}</p>}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>

      {ctaText && (
        <div class="how-act reveal">
          <a
            class="btn btn--band-accent"
            href={ctaHref}
            target={ctaExternal ? '_blank' : undefined}
            rel={ctaExternal ? 'noopener noreferrer' : undefined}
            data-field="hero.cta1.text"
            data-href-field="hero.cta1.href"
          >{ctaText}</a>
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  </section>
)}
<style>
  /* The espresso ground, the amber eyebrow, the bone .h2 and the --bone-soft
     body copy all arrive from the wrapper's one `band-dark` class; this file
     must not restate any of them.

     What IS overridden at section level is the block rhythm. The design insets
     this band by 88px, tighter than the shared section rhythm, and it scales
     down on the wrapper's own curve so it never crushes on a phone. The rule is
     (0,2,0) once Astro scopes it, so it out-specifies the wrapper's
     `html[data-page="BL"] section` at (0,1,2) on class count, without
     !important.

     The design's own `margin-top:96px` is carried too, on the same curve.
     GalleryBL renders directly above with the design's bare bottom (its
     `.gal` is `padding: … 92px 0 0`) and hands the gap to this band by name,
     so without it the mosaic butts flush against the espresso edge. A margin
     rather than padding, so the coloured ground still starts below the gap. */
  .how-band { margin-top: clamp(56px, 7.6vw, 96px); padding: clamp(56px, 7.6vw, 88px) 0; }

  /* The eyebrow is owner-typed and set in 10px caps at .42em tracking: one
     unbroken word would otherwise be the widest thing on the page (R8). */
  .kick { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  /* Design: margin .26em 0 50px. The wrapper's .h2 is a :where() rule, so this
     wins on specificity without !important. */
  .how-h2 { margin: 0.26em 0 50px; }

  /* derive-content-defaults ships how.tag on every site and NO how.headline, so
     "eyebrow, then straight into the grid" is the DEFAULT state rather than an
     edge case — and the h2's own margins are what normally open that gap.
     Adjacency, so it fires only when the headline is absent. */
  .kick + .how-grid { margin-top: 34px; }

  /* Design: repeat(3, minmax(0,1fr)) / gap 42px. minmax(0,1fr) rather than 1fr
     so a long unbroken word in a step cannot widen its track and push the page
     sideways. */
  .how-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 42px;
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }
  /* min-width:0 as well as the minmax() above: a grid item's default min-width
     is auto, so one unbroken word would still widen its own track. */
  .how-step { list-style: none; min-width: 0; }

  /* Design: 42x42, full circle, FILLED amber, #241A12 numeral, Fraunces 17px.
     The face and the tabular figures come from the wrapper's .fig; the colour
     is restated because the band rule would paint it bone on amber. Two classes
     deliberately — see the note in the frontmatter. */
  .how-num.fig {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    width: 42px;
    height: 42px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background-color: var(--band-accent);
    color: var(--band);
    font-size: 17px;
  }

  /* Design: Fraunces 400, 25px, margin 18px 0 10px, bone. The colour is not
     optional — the wrapper's shared h1-h4 reset paints --ink, which on this
     ground is invisible. The design states NO line-height anywhere above this
     h3, so in the design document it computes to `normal`; PageBL's shared
     heading rule is 1.08, which would set a two-line title tighter than it was
     drawn. `normal` is therefore restated verbatim rather than a number of our
     own — FaqBL does exactly this for its own untouched h3. Qualified with the
     section class to beat that reset. */
  .how-band .how-step h3 {
    font-family: var(--disp);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 25px;
    line-height: normal;
    margin: 18px 0 10px;
    color: var(--bone);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* Qualified with the parent class so it beats the wrapper's bare-element
     paragraph reset, which is (0,1,1). Size, leading and the --bone-soft colour
     are already exactly the design's, via .body on a band. */
  .how-band .how-step p.body { margin: 0; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  .how-act { margin-top: 50px; }

  /* R8 · this section owns its own grid. Three tracks are the design, and they
     go straight to ONE at 900px rather than through a two-up: at 900 each track
     is about 245px, a ~30-character measure for a four-line step, and three
     steps in two tracks strand a lone orphan on the second row. Stacked, the
     paragraph takes a measure cap instead: a step should not run to 110
     characters just because its column is now the whole page. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .how-grid { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 32px; }
    .how-h2 { margin-bottom: 40px; }
    .how-band .how-step p.body { max-width: 62ch; }
  }
  /* 520 · tighten the stack. Nothing here may push the page sideways: the
     overflow-wrap rules above and the minmax/min-width tracks cover the text,
     and the action goes full-bleed below. */
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .how-grid { gap: 26px; }
    .how-h2 { margin: 0.26em 0 32px; }
    .kick + .how-grid { margin-top: 24px; }
    .how-num.fig { width: 38px; height: 38px; font-size: 16px; }
    .how-band .how-step h3 { font-size: 21px; margin: 15px 0 9px; }
    .how-act { margin-top: 34px; }
    /* Full-bleed action: the wrapper pins .btn to white-space: nowrap, which
       would push the viewport sideways on a long owner-typed label. */
    .how-act .btn { width: 100%; white-space: normal; }
  }
</style>
